{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLqW889zGrW2qtPQyjmk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed it: `limit(2**(1/x), x, 0, dir='-')` now returns `0`. The right-hand limit still gives `oo`, and `limit(x**(1/x), x, oo)` still gives `1`.\n\n**Cause:** in `sympy/series/limits.py`, `Limit.doit` falls back to `pow_heuristics` when it can't expand a power. That helper called `limit(...)` without passing on the direction, so every inner limit used the default `dir='+'`. For `2**(1/x)` at `0-` it worked out the limit of `log(2)/x` from the right, which is `+oo`, and so returned `oo`.\n\n**Fix:** `pow_heuristics` now takes a `dir` argument, and all four of its inner `limit` calls pass it through. The one place that calls it passes the current direction.\n\n**Your second example:** `limit(2**x, x, -oo)` already returns `0` in this checkout, before and after my change, so I couldn't reproduce that part here. It may have been fixed after 1.13.3.\n\n**Tests:** `sympy/series/tests/test_limits.py` and `test_gruntz.py` pass (211 passed, 3 skipped, 6 xfailed). I didn't add a regression test because the task rules don't allow editing test files."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1311,"cache_read_input_tokens":22045,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1311},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"735912b1-3663-44f8-89dc-8ea83d6865b8","uuid":"dfd070ea-d2f4-4518-b793-a12fcb67905b","timestamp":"2026-11-24T00:51:46.327Z","request_id":"req_011ChLqW7rHeopJ9An7E16yH"}
